response:
 Here we have $n=2$ and the matrix is 
\[\mathbf{A}=\begin{pmatrix}  -1 & 2 \\  3 i & 4 \end{pmatrix}.\] The entry in the bottom left position is not an integer, so we will have to rationalize the denominator.  Hence,
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^2&=\begin{pmatrix}  7 & -2  \\  6 &  13 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^4&=\mathbf{A}^2\cdot\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  85 & -18  \\  30 &  181 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^6&=\mathbf{A}^2\cdot\mathbf{A}^4=\begin{pmatrix}  -259 &   6  \\  90 & 2530 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*} 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^3&=\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  -7 & 14  \\  81 & 124 \end{pmatrix} \\
-3\mathbf{A}&=\begin{pmatrix}  3 & -6  \\  -9 & -12 \end{pmatrix} 
\end{align*}

Consider finding a linear combination of $-\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{A}^2$ that is as close as possible to minimizing the $F_1$ entry of $-\mathbf{A}$.  There are many possible coefficients that are integers.  The $2$ in $-14$ with the $14$ in $14$ gives a difference of $28$ in the first entry.  But $3$ in $316$ and $9$ in $165$ gives a difference of $151$.  With $-7$ in $-80$, no combination between $-7$ and $31$ is possible that would be as close as $151$.

Therefore, using the result from above,
\begin{align*}
432\mathbf{A}&\equiv\mathbf{0} \\
38\mathbf{A}^{4}&\equiv0.75 \mathbf{A}^2\\
3\mathbf{A}^{6}&\equiv\mathbf{A}.
\end{align*}

Hence, adding all is equal follows
\begin{align*}
3\mathbf{A}^{6} + 432\mathbf{A} &\equiv \mathbf{A} + 0.75\mathbf{A}^2 \\
\mathbf{A}^{6} &\equiv 143\mathbf{A} - 0.25\mathbf{A}^2 \\
\mathbf{A}^{6} &= 143\mathbf{A} - \frac{1}{4}\mathbf{A}^2
\end{align*}

The answer is thus $(\boxed{143},\boxed{-1/4})$.
</answer>

